Do the call-and-response NOW before advancing:
SAY: &quot;Superpower activate?&quot;
CLASS: &quot;ACTIVATE!&quot;

SAY: &quot;Good. Now here&apos;s the question that scientists spent DECADES trying to answer:&quot;
SAY: &quot;How does a computer — something with no brain, no heart, no eyes — how does it LEARN?&quot;

Advance slowly. Let them sit with the question.

MINDSET SHIFT FOR PRESENTER: Module 1 is the hardest conceptually. But you have two great analogies (baby brain + puppy game) that will make it completely clear. Trust the process. Don&apos;t over-explain. The activities do the teaching for you.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" dirty="0"/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="4" name="Slide Number Placeholder 3"/><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="sldNum" sz="quarter" idx="10"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr/><p:txBody><a:bodyPr/><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:fld id="{F7021451-1387-4CA6-816F-3879F97B5CBC}" type="slidenum"><a:rPr lang="en-US"/><a:t>8</a:t></a:fld><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US"/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp></p:spTree><p:extLst><p:ext uri="{BB962C8B-B14F-4D97-AF65-F5344CB8AC3E}"><p14:creationId xmlns:p14="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2010/main" val="1024086991"/></p:ext></p:extLst></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:notes>